\par\par \b \uc2  PRESS DIGEST - Wall Street Journal - June 18. \b0\par\par\uc2 446 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Juni 1998\par 09:13\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, June 18 (Reuters) - The following stories were reported in Thursday's electronic version of the Wall Street Journal: \par \par \uc2  * The \b \highlight22\uc2 United States intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to support the yen. The \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2 ' yen purchases marked its first move in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets since August 1995 and signalled that it believes Tokyo can't regain the confidence of financial markets without help. Japan also bought yen and promised to take more aggressive measures to fix its economy. The dollar traded late in New York at 136.64 yen, down from 143.33 Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  * The Dow Jones industrials rose 164.17, or 1.89 percent, to 8829.46, in the year's second-highest percentage increase. \par \par \uc2  * Raytheon  tapped Daniel Burnham, AlliedSignal vice chairman, to succeed CEO Dennis Picard, ending a long search at the defence contractor. The choice of Burnham also clears up succession questions at AlliedSignal. \par \par \uc2  * The U.S. economy continues to grow strongly, aided by high employment and low inflation, but the South and Southwest haven't been keeping pace with other regions, the Fed said. \par \par \uc2  * Greenspan and Rubin pledged to try to resolve their differences over updating financial-services regulation to help the Senate to pass legislation. \par \par \uc2  * Renters Choice  agreed to pay $900 million for the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Britain's Thorn , a move that would make Renters Choice one of the largest operators of rent-to-own stores. \par \par \uc2  * China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  adjusted interest rates to prop up the yuan and the Hong Kong dollar. \par \par \uc2  * The FCC says cash-strapped companies will return many of the wireless licenses awarded in its 1996 auction, leaving hundreds of the permits unused at least until next year. \par \par \uc2  * Oracle  topped profit expectations for its fiscal fourth quarter. Its net rose 12 percent amid rebounding demand for its database software and services. \par \par \uc2  * AT&T  has talked with AOL  about entering a broad-based marketing and technology alliance, according to people familiar with the situation. \par \par \uc2  * GM  is battling the UAW more aggressively and more publicly than it has in previous strikes, trying to keep control of how the issues are framed. \par \par \uc2  * The SEC is policing companies that don't account for so-called cheap stock, or low-priced options granted just before IPOs, in prospectuses. \par \par \uc2  * New Orleans sued two big Wall Street firms that worked on a city bond issue now under regulators' scrutiny, entering the fray over alleged yield-burning improprieties in muni bonds. \par \par \uc2  * Japan's auto makers say they don't plan to capitalise on the yen's weakness by cutting prices in the U.S. \par \par \uc2  ((New York Newsdesk 212 859-1610)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du6i02rco\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
