\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS-US and Japan  \highlight22\uc2 intervene \highlight\uc2  to salvage ailing yen. \b0\par\par\uc2 811 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Juni 1998\par 15:33\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, June 17 (Reuters) - The central banks of the United States and Japan came to the yen's rescue on Wednesday, selling dollars for yen and driving the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  down by six percent from the near-eight-year high seen on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  U.S. Treasury Secretary Robert Rubin said the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in co-\b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  with their Japanese counterparts, and added that Washington was prepared to continue to co-operate in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. \par \par \uc2  There were no signs that it was a joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  effort by all the Group of Seven industrial nations, with officials at the central banks of Germany, Britain, Italy, France and Canada declining to comment. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders in London said only the U.S. Federal Reserve and Bank of Japan were involved. \par \par \uc2  The move took the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets entirely by surprise. G7 leaders, particularly the U.S. and Japan, have repeatedly expressed worries about the yen's weakness and the potentially dangerous impact of this on Asia's financial turmoil, but they had shown no signs of willingness to step into the market to rescue the yen. \par \par \uc2  "This signals a shift in the U.S. administration's stance," said Rob Hayward, an economist at the Bank of America in London. \par \par \uc2  Until now, the U.S. has been emphasising the need to improve Japan's financial system and the general state of its economy, officially declared to be in recession after data on Friday showed GDP had shrunk for two straight quarters. \par \par \uc2  Pessimism over the world's second-biggest economy persists as the government's repeated efforts to galvanise it with measures such as public works spending, tax cuts and deregulation have so far failed to give activity a substantial boost. \par \par \uc2  The dollar's nose-dive began as the North American trading session was kicking off for the day and dollar/yen slid more than four percent from its session high in a matter of minutes. \par \par \uc2  By 1325 GMT, it had slid even further to a low of 137.25 yen, down from the high of 146.75 yen reached on Tuesday which was the highest since August 1990. \par \par \uc2  It was the first time the U.S. authorities had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on behalf of the yen since February 1992. The Bank of Japan has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on two occasions amid the yen's slide in recent months, once last December and again this April. \par \par \uc2  Each time, the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  succeeded in pushing dollar/yen down by about 4.5 percent but it recovered in a matter of days. \par \par \uc2  "If the U.S. want to make this move stick, Rubin has to be explicit and say that this is a complete policy change," said Chris Furness, senior market strategist at 4CAST. \par \par \uc2  "The Fed have let it be known to banks that they want the world to know what they've done. Estimates at this stage are that they have sold at least $2 billionagainst the yen - \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is thus significant," Furness said. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. and Japan joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  came on the eve of an expected visit to Japan by U.S. Deputy Treasury Secretary Lawrence Summers, New York Federal Reserve president William McDonough and officials of the State Department. \par \par \uc2  The Treasury Department said Summers will meet senior Japanese officials and participate in a joint meeting on Saturday with officials from the G7 countries, with their discussions focusing on the economic situation in Japan and the rest of Asia. \par \par \uc2  The Japanese government is under pressure from nations around the world to pull its economy out of the doldrums so that it can act as an engine for neighbouring countries suffering from the financial crisis which has ravaged the region for nearly a year. \par \par \uc2  While a weak yen makes Japanese manufacturers' exports more competitive, other Asian nations are averse to it because it makes theirs less so. \par \par \uc2  China gave the financial markets a nasty jolt on Wednesday when one of its senior officials said Beijing could be forced to devalue its yuan \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  if the tumbling yen hit Chinese exports too hard. \par \par \uc2  Such a warning was alarming coming from China, which had been regarded as something of a stronghold. Until now, Beijing had repeatedly declared it will not devalue the yuan. \par \par \uc2  Some pointed out that the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to boost the yen was also taking place just before U.S. President Bill Clinton's visit to China. \par \par \uc2  "The timing of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  looks like it could be pretty closely linked to China," said one trader at a British bank. "The Clinton administration looks like it is taking on board China's protestations about the weak yen, and this \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  times in quite nicely with Clinton's visit to China next week." \par \par \uc2  ((Chizu Nomiyama, London newsroom, +44 171-542-6721, uk.\b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 .news@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du6h02pua\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
