\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS-Japan and US agree on moves to halt yen drop. \b0\par\par\uc2  By William Mallard \par \uc2 886 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Juni 1998\par 10:45\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, June 17 (Reuters) - Japan and the United States agreed on Wednesday that the two economic superpowers had to act together to stop a nosedive in the Japanese yen that had sparked fears of an Asia-wide depression that could turn global. \par \par \uc2  Their promises were backed up by an earlier round of concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market, estimated by traders at $2 billion, that drove the yen sharply higher. \par \par \uc2  The statement by the two leaders came a few hours after Japan's parliament passed a special 4.65 trillion yen ($32.7 billion) supplementary budget that is part of a record 16.65 trillion yen economic stimulus package announced in April. \par \par \uc2  The announcement by President Bill Clinton and Prime Minister Ryutaro Hashimoto appeared to end for now an increasingly bitter war of words about what Japan should do to stimulate its long-suffering economy and rescue a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  showing no end to its decline. \par \par \uc2  "The president and I are delighted to see that the United States and Japan have cooperated in the exchange markets to support a strong, stable yen," Hashimoto said in a statement after a telephone talk with Clinton. \par \par \uc2  The statement came a day ahead of a hastily arranged visit by U.S. Deputy Treasury Secretary Lawrence Summers that will include a meeting of finance officials from the Group of Seven industrialised nations. \par \par \uc2  Financial analysts had said that if Summers came to Japan without some sort of deal, he threatened to worsen the situation. \par \par \uc2  A Chinese official said earlier on Wednesday that the sliding yen could force China to devalue the yuan, the first such warning of the threats posed by the yen's slide. China has blamed both Japan and the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2  for the growing crisis. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  marks a reversal for Washington, which had said that only an improvement in the Japanese economy would halt the yen's fall to a string of eight-year lows. \par \par \uc2  It was the first time U.S. authorities \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on behalf of the yen since February 1992. \par \par \uc2  U.S. Treasury Secretary Robert Rubin said the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  took place "in the context of Japan's plans to strengthen its economy" and said the United States was ready to continue the action if needed. \par \par \uc2  Hashimoto and other Japanese government officials have meanwhile been stressing the need to wait for the now-enacted economic stimulus package, saying it will boost the economy by at least two percent and ensure a recovery by autumn. \par \par \uc2  But many economists doubt the fiscal stimulus will do more than give growth a temporary boost, while failing to resolve the basic problems plaguing Japan's banking system and economy. \par \par \uc2  Bank of Japan Policy Board member Susumu Taketomi said on Wednesday that resolving financial institutions' problem loans was the key to restoring confidence, a point later echoed in Hashimoto's statement. \par \par \uc2  "The government of Japan will expeditiously restructure the financial system, including the prompt disposal of bad assets and the abandonment of the "convoy" system (of protecting problem companies), Hashimoto said in his statement. \par \par \uc2  "We will also accelerate the implementation of the fiscal stimulus package and reform both corporate and individual income tax structures," he said. \par \par \uc2  BOJ governor Masaru Hayami, in a rare gesture, issued a statement on Tuesday urging banks to disclose their internal views on the health of potentially problem loans, to which private bankers responded sceptically. \par \par \uc2  Finance Minister Hikaru Matsunaga sided with the banks, although he said he shares the BOJ's view that the problem loans are choking the economy. \par \par \uc2  Critics said the rare statement by the BOJ may be an indication that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's options to help the economy or support the excessively weak yen were limited, with Japan's official discount rate is already at a record low 0.5 percent. \par \par \uc2  While the release of minutes of the BOJ's April 24 policy meeting revealed that many had pushed for cautious consideration of easier credit, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  decided last week to keep the current monetary policy unchanged. \par \par \uc2  In a sign of the persistent worries about Japan's banking system, the share price of the Long-Term Credit Bank of Japan plunged on Wednesday as rumours about financial difficulties - denied by the bank - set off wave after wave of selling. \par \par \uc2  Matsunaga told parliament no Japanese bank was currently in danger of failure, but such assurances are unlikely to calm Japan's jitters. \par \par \uc2  Efforts are underway by the ruling Liberal Democratic Party (LDP) to resolve the bad loan woes of the nation's banks, and senior party officials have raised the idea of using more public loans to help the banks besides the 30 trillion yen currently set aside for that purpose. \par \par \uc2  "We will tell him (Summers) that Japan is doing its best to solve the bad loan problem," government spokesman Kanezo Muraoka said earlier on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Markets, meanwhile, were also waiting for word of whether Japan's top financial diplomat, Eisuke Sakakibara, and Summers' counterpart, was going to stay in his post for another year. \par \par \uc2  His post was not among personnel changes announced on Wednesday, but officials said more reshuffling could come after parliament concluded on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  ((Tokyo Newsroom +81-3 3432 8018 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du6h02pjx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
