\par\par \b \uc2  U.S. stocks storm higher as central banks boost Japan's yen. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Marjorie Olster \par \uc2 823 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Juni 1998\par 23:35\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, June 17 (Reuters) - U.S. stocks soared on Wednesday after \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  ignited a big recovery in the long suffering Japanese yen, defusing the threat of a vicious circle of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  devaluations in Asia. \par \par \uc2  The Dow Jones industrial average rose 164.17 points, or almost 1.9 percent, at 8,829.46, recapturing a large portion of Monday's 207-point loss. \par \par \uc2  In the broad market, advancing issues beat declines 2,268 to 885 on heavy volume of 721 million shares on the New York Stock Exchange. \par \par \uc2  The Nasdaq composite index jumped 23.28 points, or 1.3 percent, to 1,776.40. \par \par \uc2  "Last week, there was fear of the collapsing yen and competitive devaluations leading to the possibility of open-ended depression," said Michael Metz, a managing director at CIBC Oppenheimer & Corp. \par \par \uc2  "That's not solved, but it's no longer open-ended," he said. "That laissez-faire attitude was a cause of great anxiety and now you have a huge sigh of relief." \par \par \uc2  In a surprise move that may have kept the Asian economic crisis from worsening, the United States and Japan raided \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to support Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , buying yen and selling dollars. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  knocked the dollar down to 136.75 yen in late New York trading from 143.35 yen on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The last time the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to help the yen was in 1992 and it decided again to step in after the Japanese unit fell this week to an eight-year low against the dollar. European central banks did not join the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  that traders estimated at $2 billion. \par \par \uc2  U.S. Treasury Secretary Robert Rubin said the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  took place "in the context of Japan's plans to strengthen its economy" and said the United States was ready to continue the action if needed. \par \par \uc2  The yen's rally calmed Asian nerves after a key Chinese official warned that a crumbling yen could force Beijing to devalue its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the yuan. It was the first time that a top Chinese official has commented openly about devaluation, which could set off the next \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  crisis in the Pac Rim. \par \par \uc2  The yen's recovery halted, for the time being, the flight to quality that had driven investors to the bond market, and sent long-term bond interest rates to record lows this week. \par \par \uc2  The key 30-year Treasury bond was plunged 1-6/32 and its yield, which moves in the opposite direction, jumped to 5.74 percent from 5.66 percent at Tuesday's close. \par \par \uc2  The biggest percentage gainers on the NYSE were Asian-linked equities. Asia Pacific was up 7/8 at 6-5/8, Thaifund Inc. rose 15/16 at 6-9/16, Greater China Fund was ahead 5/8 at 7-1/16, Korea Fund rose 1 at 6-1/2. \par \par \uc2  Guy Truicko, a portfolio manager at Unity Management, said the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  move could be a harbinger that Japan is about to embark on new reforms in return for the U.S. support. \par \par \uc2  Analysts agreed that propping up the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was addressing the symptom, not the cause, of the malady. \par \par \uc2  Philip Orlando, chief investment officer of Value Line's Asset Management division, said the market would be more impressed by a concerted Japanese effort to cut taxes, increase stimulus spending and lower interest rates to boost both the Japanese and regional economy. \par \par \uc2  A continued recovery in crude oil prices lifted the stocks of oil companies. \par \par \uc2  Oil prices gained 62 cents at $12.60 a barrel on the New York Mercantile Exchange on news of production cuts by Persian Gulf producers to boost prices. \par \par \uc2  Chevron Corp. rose 2-7/16 to 83-11/16. Exxon Corp. was up 1-7/16 at 71. \par \par \uc2  The Internet stock sector was gripped by speculative takeover fever once again following a published report that the world's biggest online service, America Online Inc., snubbed a takeover offer from telephone giant AT&T Corp. \par \par \uc2  America Online was up 5-1/16 at 93-13/16, and AT&T rose 1-1/16 to 63. \par \par \uc2  Hewlett-Packard Co. was the lone Dow index component that ended lower. The stock fell 15/16 to 58-1/2 after Merrill Lynch downgraded its near-term rating on HP to neutral from accumulate and its long-term rating to accumulate from buy. \par \par \uc2  The Standard & Poor's composite index of 500 stocks rose 19.51 points to 1,107.10. The American Stock Exchange index gained 6.61 to 695.27. \par \par \uc2  The NYSE Composite index of all listed common stocks rose 9.73 to 568.65. The average share was up 79 cents. \par \par \uc2  The Wilshire Associates Equity Index - the market value of NYSE, American and Nasdaq issues - was 10,386.988, up 172.902 or 1.69 percent. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du6h02oxu\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
