\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS - U.S. comes to rescue of weak Japanese yen. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Knut Engelmann \par \uc2 769 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Juni 1998\par 21:14\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  WASHINGTON, June 17 (Reuters) - The United States rode to the rescue of the battered Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Wednesday in a dramatic shift from its recent hands-off approach amid mounting concern over Japan's slumping economy. \par \par \uc2  Joining forces with Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the Treasury Department instructed the New York Federal Reserve to spend an estimated $2 billion to boost the value of the yen against the dollar - the first U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on behalf of the yen in more than six years. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is when central banks buy or sell a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the open market to affect its value. \par \par \uc2  The value of the yen immediately surged to 137 to the dollar after the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , compared with 142 yen just before. The Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  had fallen to its lowest level in eight years recently on fears that the ailing Japanese economy would spark another round of Asian financial shocks. \par \par \uc2  Treasury Secretary Robert Rubin said Washington stood ready to continue supporting the yen if needed. \par \par \uc2  News of the action hit nervous \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets only hours before Rubin's deputy Lawrence Summers was due to leave for Tokyo to meet senior Japanese officials for hastily convened crisis talks on the economic situation in Japan and elsewhere in troubled Asia. \par \par \uc2  In Tokyo, Summers was also due to attend a meeting of deputy finance ministers from the Group of Seven industrial nations. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  appeared limited to U.S. and Japanese authorities, without a hand from other big industrialised countries. Britain said the action was a U.S.-Japanese initiative and wished it every success. \par \par \uc2  "This is a clear shift in U.S. policy and it probably indicates that the U.S. has received some concessions from Japan," said Henry Willmore, senior economist at Barclays Capital in New York. \par \par \uc2  President Bill Clinton said the action was taken to show that Washington supported Japan as it takes steps to revive its economy. Clinton said he had spoken with Japanese Prime Minister Ryutaro Hashimoto shortly before midnight Tuesday to express U.S. support for Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  "I was very encouraged by the Prime Minister's statement that he intends to pursue aggressive reform of their banking institutions and intends to do the things that were necessary to get the economy going again," Clinton said in response to a question at the White House. \par \par \uc2  Japan's economy, the second largest in the world after the United States, has tumbled into recession this year, shrinking at an annual rate of 5.3 percent in the first quarter of 1998. \par \par \uc2  Worried that the Japanese crisis could spread elsewhere in Asia and ultimately affect the U.S. economy, Washington has repeatedly called on Tokyo to do more to revive its economy. But it has been frustrated with Japan's reluctance to commit to specific plans. \par \par \uc2  An additional factor in the U.S. decision to go to Japan's rescue was fear that the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  slide would force its huge neighbour China to devalue its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . China had warned that it could not withstand the economic pressure from the Japanese situation much longer. \par \par \uc2  "There's one other reason we're doing this - a very important reason - and that's the Chinese," said a U.S. administration official, who asked not to be identified. \par \par \uc2  The official said that Washington wanted to avoid at all costs a devaluation of the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the yuan, because that could have set off a chain reaction throughout Asia. \par \par \uc2  The sagging yen and the decline of Japan's economy have put additional economic pressure on already struggling nations across the Asian continent. \par \par \uc2  Exporters in countries such as South Korea, Indonesia or Thailand - at the receiving end of multibillion dollar bailout deals - need the Japanese market to sell their products, but are unable to do so since the weak yen makes their products more expensive for the Japanese to buy. \par \par \uc2  White House spokesman Michael McCurry suggested the yen \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  may have reduced the pressure on Beijing to devalue its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Asked about reports China had felt renewed pressures to devalue, he told a briefing "that may have been before some of the things that we've been discussing in the last few minutes." \par \par \uc2  "The president has noted approvingly the attitude Chinese authorities have taken with respect to Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," he added. Clinton joined by Rubin and other top U.S. officials are due to visit China next week. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2  has not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to support the yen's value since 1992. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du6h02ohy\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
