\par\par \b \uc2  Rubin roils yen as  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  hopes fade. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Knut Engelmann \par \uc2 761 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Juni 1998\par 02:19\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  WASHINGTON, June 11 (Reuters) - Hopes the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2  might help Japan stem the decline of the ailing yen faded on Thursday after U.S. Treasury Secretary Robert Rubin dismissed market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a mere short-term instrument. \par \par \uc2  In his most explicit comments to date on what should be done about the sagging Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Rubin suggested the only cure to the yen's weakness was restoring economic growth in Japan, the world's second-largest economy. \par \par \uc2  Financial markets took Rubin's remarks to mean that he was unwilling to instruct the Federal Reserve to buy yen on the open market to boost its value, prompting a sell-off in the yen that pushed it to its lowest level against the dollar in almost eight years. \par \par \uc2  "The weakness of the yen reflects the economic conditions in Japan, and can only be remedied by restoring economic strength in Japan," Rubin told the Senate Finance Committee. \par \par \uc2  He later added \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was merely a "temporary tool and not a fundamental solution," remarks that further sped the yen's fall. After apparently realising that his statement had roiled the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, Rubin insisted he did not want to "mislead" markets by ruling out \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  completely. \par \par \uc2  "We will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  when appropriate or not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  when it's not appropriate. It's always a tool that's available," he added. That clarification briefly halted the yen's slide, but it quickly resumed its decline and was last trading at just above 144.00 to the U.S. dollar. \par \par \uc2  Analysts agreed \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to bolster the yen would be pointless since relative \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  values were merely reflecting current realities: a Japanese economy stuck in recession and a U.S. economy enjoying its best performance in a generation by most accounts. \par \par \uc2  "There's a lot of reasons why \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would not be effective now, most importantly because of fundamentals. The outlook right now just favours a strong dollar," said Tom Gallagher, a political economist at Lehman Brothers. \par \par \uc2  Mounting concern over Japan's ability to pull itself out of its economic mess just as much of the rest of Asia has been hit by a financial crisis has scared investors around the globe and contributed to sharp losses in Asian stock and bond markets. \par \par \uc2  Washington, along with its western partners in the Group of Seven top industrial nations, has repeatedly called on Tokyo to boost domestic demand, strengthen its banking sector and open up its markets to boost growth - so far without much success. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. administration is afraid that bowing to Tokyo's demands for joint \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  now could take the heat off the Japanese government to implement economic reforms. \par \par \uc2  What's more, a strong dollar helps to keep a lid on U.S. inflation, lowering the risk of an interest rate rise by the Fed aimed at dampening the torrid pace of economic growth. \par \par \uc2  "From a political point of view, the administration could be blamed for any subsequent rate hike," Gallagher noted. \par \par \uc2  Fed Chairman Alan Greenspan on Wednesday warned Congress the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would raise rates unless the red-hot economy slows down, even though he added that so far at least he saw few signs of overheating or a developing wage-price spiral. \par \par \uc2  It has been more than six years since the Fed, acting on Treasury's behalf, last sold dollars and bought yen on the open market. It last \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets in August 1995, when it bought dollars and sold yen in a bid to stem the greenback's fall against major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Since then, the picture has been all but turned around: the dollar has risen by more than 60 yen, or 75 percent, and now it is the yen causing a headache in most parts of the world. \par \par \uc2  U.S. exporters and car makers are ringing the alarm bells over falling sales to Japan. In Asia, fears are mounting that Japan's malaise will make it more difficult for hard-hit countries such as South Korea, Thailand or Indonesia to boost their own slumping economies by raising exports. \par \par \uc2  Already, the gloom has spread to China amid growing concern over Beijing's ability to defend its own \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the yuan, should the yen continue its free fall. \par \par \uc2  Rubin, stressing that Tokyo's problems had become a world-wide concern, called the situation "troubling". \par \par \uc2  He is due to join President Bill Clinton on a state visit to China later this month, but has no plans to visit Japan. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du6c0379k\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
