\par\par \b \uc2  Yen on the ropes in Europe as Japan gloom deepens. \b0\par\par\uc2 553 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Juni 1998\par 09:51\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, June 12 (Reuters) - The yen made a weak start in Europe on Friday, battered by data showing a worsening economic picture in Japan and fading expectations of concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  action to support the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Profit-taking took the yen off its near eight-year lows against the dollar and 5-1/2 year lows against the mark seen overnight. But today's gloomy Japanese GDP data point to continued weakness for the yen. \par \par \uc2  "The GDP data was worse than expected and the Japanese economy is looking in quite big trouble," said James McKay, European economist at Commonwealth Bank of Australia in London. \par \par \uc2  Japan's first quarter GDP fell a real 1.3 percent from the previous quarter, or an annualised fall of 5.3 percent. Combined with contraction in the fourth quarter of 1997, the data show Japan is technically in recession. \par \par \uc2  "The Japanese GDP figures this morning just served to confirm what we already knew. We are not going to see recovery out of Japan for the next year," said Bronwyn Curtis, chief international economist at Nomura International in London. \par \par \uc2  The dollar climbed to 144.72 yen overnight, its highest point since August 1990, before pre-weekend profit-taking pared gains back to 143.98/08 by 0745 GMT. \par \par \uc2  The yen also suffered against the mark, slipping to its lowest level since November 1992, and against the pound where it tested lows not seen since September 1992. \par \par \uc2  Japan's economic woes fuelled the yen bears already excited by Thursday's comments by U.S. Treasury Secretary Robert Rubin. \par \par \uc2  The yen sank when he dismissed \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a short-term tool and suggested the key to a stronger yen was restoring economic growth in Japan. \par \par \uc2  "Rubin is letting Japan hang out to dry until they come up with something substantial to turn the economy around," said Tony Norfield, chief economist at ABN-Amro in London. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said Rubin's comments killed off expectations that the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2  or the G7 would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to boost the yen any time soon, undermining that key support for the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  However, analysts say the United states will not be able to sit on its hands for much longer as Asia instability begins to take a bigger bite out of both the U.S. and the global economies. \par \par \uc2  "The 1.8 percent slide in Wall Street yesterday serves as a strong warning that Japan's problems cannot be ignored," said Paul Meggyesi, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  economist at Deutsche Bank. \par \par \uc2  "And simply telling Japan to put its own house in order is scarcely conducive to fostering a sense of stability." \par \par \uc2  The dollar/yen gains served to support the dollar against the mark which ticked up to a seven-week high of 1.8106 marks overnight, a level it looked set to retest in morning Europe. \par \par \uc2  By 0820 GMT, dollar/mark was trading at 1.8089/94 from 1.8035/40 marks late Thursday in Europe. \par \par \uc2  The next data focus is on U.S. May producer prices at 1230 GMT. An average of analysts' expectations point to a 0.1 percent rise in both the headline and core figure. \par \par \uc2  ((Andrew Reierson, London newsroom +44 171 542 6745, uk.\b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 .news@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du6c02x23\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
