\par\par \b \uc2  Rubin says U.S. shares Japan's concern on yen. \b0\par\par\uc2 404 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Juni 1998\par 03:23\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, June 9 (Reuters) - U.S. Treasury Secretary Robert Rubin on Tuesday said the United States shared Japan's concern about the weak yen as the yen hovered at multiyear lows. \par \par \uc2  "Our view is the same as it has been for many months, we shared and do share Japan's concern about the weakness of the yen," Rubin told reporters before delivering a speech at a gala dinner here. \par \par \uc2  Rubin added, "It is extremely important that Japan begin again to get back to a track of solid domestic demand-led growth, and in that respect Japan needs to deal with fiscal policy which as you know they have already done or are at least in the process of doing." \par \par \uc2  Rubin also stressed that Japan's economic recovery would hold the key for the revival in the entire region. \par \par \uc2  "I think that the adverse economic conditions in Japan affect the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and all of that does affect conditions in Asia which is why it is so important for Japan to get back on track," Rubin said. \par \par \uc2  Earlier this week the U.S. dollar soared past the psychologically key 140 yen level as investors continued to lose confidence in the Tokyo's ability to bolster its economy. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers on Tuesday said even veiled threats from Japanese officials that Tokyo's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem the yen's sharp losses failed to stem yen sales. \par \par \uc2  At 2128 EDT dollar/yen stood at 140.40. \par \par \uc2  Rubin said Tokyo must tackle fundamental issues like banking reform in order to restore confidence in the yen since "the key to what any \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  does is fundamentals." \par \par \uc2  But Rubin stopped short of commenting on whether the United States might extend any assistance to Japan. Asked whether there might be a time when the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2  would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to buoy the yen, Rubin said with a laugh: "We never comment on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as you know." The Bank of Japan surprised \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets with massive dollar sales shortly before the Easter holiday but the effect was short-lived. The yen has weakened considerably since then. \par \par \uc2  Turning to other nations in Asia, Rubin urged them "to continue to implement strong reform program in accordance with their arrangement with the International Monetary Fund." \par \par \uc2  ((Svea Herbst-Bayliss, N.A. Treasury Desk, 212-859-1639 )). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du6a03a0p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
