\par\par \b \uc2  ANALYSIS-Yen fall boxes world powers into a corner. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Swaha Pattanaik \par \uc2 901 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Juni 1998\par 14:41\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, June 8 (Reuters) - Top industrial nations face mounting problems caused by a rapidly falling yen unless they come up with a plan comparable to their major \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pacts of the last decade, the Plaza and Louvre accords. \par \par \uc2  That, at least, is the growing conviction of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, which saw the yen driven to its weakest levels in seven years on Monday, beyond 140 per dollar. \par \par \uc2  So far, there has not been any joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from countries in the Group of Seven (G7) - the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Japan, Germany, France, Italy, Britain and Canada. \par \par \uc2  While the G7 may prefer to wait until economic conditions favour a stronger yen before launching a coordinated defence of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , analysts said they could be forced to take action before then. \par \par \uc2  "I expect joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  sooner rather than later, and definitely some time in the month of June," said Martin de Blocq, sales manager at Nomura Bank in London. \par \par \uc2  "The reason they will do it is because the yen is the cause of all the turmoil in Asia and this is now hitting Russia. Stabilising dollar/yen is in the world's interests." \par \par \uc2  Although the Bank of Japan has already \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on its own this year, the G7 last pitted its combined forces against the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in 1995 when the dollar was setting record lows against the mark and post-World War Two lows below 80 yen. \par \par \uc2  While they did not succeed in turning the market around immediately, it did not take more than a couple of rounds of coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , backed up by continuous solo yen sales by the Bank of Japan, to get the dollar rising again. \par \par \uc2  The other force behind the dollar's rise of more than 60 yen - or 75 percent - since then is the U.S. government's policy favouring a strong dollar. This is a far cry from the days of Lloyd Bentsen, the previous U.S. Treasury Secretary, who was perceived to favour a weaker dollar for trade purposes. \par \par \uc2  The current U.S. policy, repeated regularly by U.S. Treasury Secretary Robert Rubin, argues that it is in America's interest for the dollar to be strong. Although it has eroded U.S. exporters' competitiveness and the value of their foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  earnings, it has also attracted more investors into the U.S. bond market and reduced the cost of financing U.S. debt. \par \par \uc2  Moreover, it has helped to keep U.S. inflation in check at a time when the economy is expanding at a strong pace. \par \par \uc2  However, such benefits may soon begin to be outweighed by the negative repercussions of the downturn in Asian economies caused by the asset market turmoil which has already been seen in the region, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  American technology companies have been dented by concern about the Asian economies. The market is already talking about further losses in the yen, towards 150 to 160 per dollar. This would further damage the economies of China, South Korea, and Taiwan, key competitors of Japan. \par \par \uc2  Against this backdrop, the U.S. may soon be forced to switch gears and join Japan in buying yen, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  "If China's economy is suffering and Chinese officials are complaining, there may be a situation when the interests of Europe, the U.S., and Japan coincide, and at that point we could see some (joint) \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," Rob Hayward, economic advisor at Bank of America in London, told Reuters Television. \par \par \uc2  Such a policy shift on the part of the U.S. would invite comparisons to the Louvre and Plaza accords of the 1980s, which represent turning points in the dollar's fortunes. \par \par \uc2  In 1985 the G7, faced with a rampant dollar, agreed on what became known as the Plaza Accord, a pact to drive the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  down. Two years later, when the dollar had become too weak, it came up with the Louvre Accord, signalling a determination to drive it back up. \par \par \uc2  To be sure, the U.S. these days could reconcile joint \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  action with its strong dollar policy by presenting it as a move to rescue the yen, not weaken the dollar per se. \par \par \uc2  The yen has weakened across the board, depreciating to five-and-a-half-year lows against the mark on Monday. By contrast, the dollar is generally firm but its rally against the mark ran out of steam some time ago. \par \par \uc2  Bank of America's Hayward said the yen had not yet fallen far enough to warrant such action, but traders said losses of another three to four percent against the dollar could trigger \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Joint \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  buying of yen could be more effective than the market currently expects given that traders are already very short of yen. \par \par \uc2  Also, there are signs in the options market that people are beginning to favour taking a chance on a yen rebound. For instance, one-month options to sell marks for yen have just come into vogue, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  "The mid-140 area is the sort of level we should be questioning if there is much rationale for a move much higher and then the market may even start to come off of its own accord," said Keith Lewis, manager, treasury markets at Amex Bank. ((London newsroom, +44 171 542 6284, uk.\b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 .news@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du6803dw2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
