\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar holds gains at cross-driven U.S. noon. \b0\par\par\uc2 573 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Mai 1998\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, May 11 (Reuters) - The dollar rose modestly against the mark at midday, propped as early profit-taking on the European crosses led the greenback to a decisive break of technical resistance against the mark, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Short covering lifted dollar/yen from lows hit on renewed \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  speculation, despite yen repurchases versus the mark and sterling. \par \par \uc2  Traders said an early rebound in sterling followed a healthy 0.7 percent rise in UK industrial production for March that briefly stemmed the tide of negative sentiment surrounding the British \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  for more than a month. \par \par \uc2  "When sterling/mark came above 2.90 that was a very psychological move and supportive for the dollar," said Hans Boman, chief spot dealer at Swedbank, who added that the dollar/mark bounce was also significant after the pair sustained weeks of downward pressure. \par \par \uc2  Dollar/mark at noon was up at 1.7770/80 from 1.7743/48 while dollar/yen rose to 132.71/74 from 132.38/48. \par \par \uc2  Sterling/mark lost 2.90 quickly, however, as dealers latched back onto safe-haven unwinding trades that have seriously pressured the pound in recent sessions. The cross was almost a pfennig off its high, near 2.8980 at noon. \par \par \uc2  "Sterling/mark is now way-offered. There's a realization that EMU is going through and it had just bounced back up to a level where people felt more comfortable selling," said a trader at a French bank in New York. \par \par \uc2  Technical dollar/mark resistance at 1.7750 became support, and was an important level on a closing basis for a broader rally toward 1.7850 in the near-term, a trader said. However, dealers said the sterling/mark turnaround and offers ahead of 1.78 made a close above the support level questionable. \par \par \uc2  The bigger picture depicted range trading as long as dollar/mark remained below the 1.80 area, Boman said. \par \par \uc2  Dollar/yen shorts squared in U.S. trade, ending a decline from comments from senior Japan official Haruhiko Kuroda. \par \par \uc2  Kuroda, director-general of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 's International Finance Bureau, fueled fears of Bank of Japan \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  after he said Japan and the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2  discussed joint action in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets on Friday. \par \par \uc2  Widespread forecasts for a higher dollar/yen also factored into the rise at noon, but the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pair was seen capped at the 133.50 level amid the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  fears and also by a preponderance of long positions already in the market. \par \par \uc2  The dollar could be underpinned by any hints of continuing economic strength in the second quarter from myriad U.S. data due this week, including April retail sales data on Wednesday, traders said. \par \par \uc2  U.S. data were in focus following Friday's report that the nation's unemployment rate hit a 28-year low at 4.3 percent, and that non-farm payrolls grew by 262,000. \par \par \uc2  Interest rate differentials between the U.S. and Germany were also highlighted after benign German inflation data reinforced speculation that a Bundesbank rate hike was not needed anytime soon. \par \par \uc2  In other trading, the dollar was higher at 1.4855/65 Swiss francs from 1.4823/33 at the open. Sterling fell to $1.6290/00 from $1.6355/65 and the greenback dipped to Canadian $1.4332/37 from C$1.4345/50. \par \par \uc2  ((-Al Yoon, 212-859-1672)). \par \par \uc2  (