\par\par \b \uc2  Mark/yen slippage mixes dollar at U.S. open. \b0\par\par\uc2 655 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Mai 1998\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, May 11 (Reuters) - The dollar began U.S. trade lower versus the yen but higher against the mark as investors exited short-yen holdings after a Japanese official emphasized the threat of joint U.S.-Japan market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Dealers said comments by Haruhiko Kuroda, director-general of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 's International Finance Bureau, saying the U.S. and Japan at the G7 meeting in London on Friday voiced readiness to jointly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , lifted the yen. \par \par \uc2  "It's weighing on dollar/yen. We should be a yen higher or so. We're getting closer to where they have drawn the line in the sand," said an Australian bank trader. \par \par \uc2  Kuroda told reporters in Tokyo overnight that a joint U.S.-Japan statement "said we are prepared to take joint (\b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) action." \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Japan \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on April 9 to sell dollars at around 133.60 yen and dealers said investors would be unwilling to push the dollar through that level for now. \par \par \uc2  "The Kuroda statement caused yen buying, but we're more or less continuing in ranges. People will hesitate to be short yen above 133," said Ben Strauss, trader at Bank Julius Baer. \par \par \uc2  The dollar opened at 132.38/48 yen, down from Friday's close at 132.75/80 and an overnight peak at 133.20. \par \par \uc2  But market players voiced doubts that the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2  would join Japan in any \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  efforts, noting Japan has gone it alone in previous efforts. The greenback has typically recovered from previous Bank of Japan selling, they added. \par \par \uc2  "Every time we get above 133, they wave that flag. That doesn't necessarily mean that anything is going to happen," said a dealer at a Japanese bank in New York. \par \par \uc2  The mark slumped against the yen, ending a strong recent rally, which helped bolster the dollar against the mark as well, dealers said. "People were long mark/yen Friday and now they are unloading some marks," the Japanese bank dealer said. \par \par \uc2  Sterling also rebounded after a week of heavy losses to the mark, augmenting the bid under dollar/mark, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The pound got a lift when Britain reported industrial production rose 0.7 percent in March, better than the consensus forecast for a 0.1-percent advance, dealers noted. \par \par \uc2  But sterling could continue to underperform amid growing belief that Britain has seen the last interest rate hike of the current cycle while rates in Germany and other continental European nations will go higher this year, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "Sterling/mark lost 10 big figures last week. This was one piece of good data. It still looks heavy," said Strauss. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the dollar could trade in a narrow range until Wednesday, when the U.S. Commerce Department will report April retail sales. \par \par \uc2  Last Friday's surprising report that U.S. unemployment fell to a 28-year low 4.3 percent in April sparked talk that the Federal Reserve could raise interest rates in coming months. The retail sales report will offer the next major clue on the U.S. economy. \par \par \uc2  "Until we get some inkling of whether or not the Fed will move, we're stuck here," Strauss said. \par \par \uc2  Dollar/yen's range is now 131.50 to 133.50, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Dollar/mark shortly after the U.S. open broke through Friday's high at 1.7750, which some dealers said could open room to 1.79. Mark/yen is nearing crucial support at 74.50, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar opened higher at 1.4823/33 Swiss francs from 1.4795/05 on Friday. Sterling was off slightly at $1.6355/65 from $1.6360/70. The greenback dipped to Canadian $1.4345/50 from C$1.4364/69. The Australian dollar opened at $0.6335/40, down from $0.6346/51 on Friday. \par \par \uc2  ((-Chris Stetkiewicz, 212-859-1664)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du5b05mzk\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
