\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  part of talks with U.S.-Sakakibara. \b0\par\par\uc2 605 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 April 1998\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, April 24 (Reuters) - Japan's top financial diplomat Eisuke Sakakibara told Reuters Television late on Friday that Japan remains in contact with the U.S. Treasury Department, including discussions on the possibility of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to boost the yen. \par \par \uc2  "Our relations with the U.S. Treasury are very good," Sakakibara said in an interview. "We continue to consult, including the possibility of \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." \par \par \uc2  Sakakibara downplayed recent expressions of frustration by his boss, Finance Minister Hikaru Matsunaga, over a perceived lack of support by the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2  for Japanese yen buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  He said he did not want to comment on Matsunaga's remarks, but stressed the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 's (MOF) contact with the Treasury, adding: "I personally will continue to talk with (Deputy Secretary) Larry Summers." \par \par \uc2  Sakakibara said \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets have been very bearish on the yen. "I think they are wrong," he said. \par \par \uc2  He said Japan's economy and its real estate markets are "more or less bottoming out". \par \par \uc2  Sakakibara warned yen bears that towards the end of the year the yen will be more likely strengthen than weaken. \par \par \uc2  "If you think in terms of six months or nine months, there will be a turnaround somewhere (for dollar/yen). Markets move on perception. Once the perception changes, the turnaround may be very quick," he said. \par \par \uc2  Asked at what point Japan might \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  again, Sakakibara said: "Our policy is not leaning against the wind but leaning with the wind." \par \par \uc2  He said remarks he made earlier this week on the possibility of selling U.S. Treasury securities were simply an explanation of the mechanics of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and were not an attack on the U.S. \par \par \uc2  He told reporters earlier this week that Japan could always sell U.S. Treasuries to fund an \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  In the interview, Sakakibara said he merely meant that to buy yen in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, Japan must sell either some of its dollar deposits or some of its Treasury holdings. \par \par \uc2  "My remarks were not meant to be anti-U.S. or anti-Treasury or anything. It was an explanation of the mechanical procedure that we have to go through," he said. \par \par \uc2  Sakakibara also played down earlier his remarks that the U.S. stock market was peaking. \par \par \uc2  He said various people have said that "there are some elements of bubbles" in U.S. equity and real estate prices. \par \par \uc2  "I wouldn't say the U.S. economy has a bubble, but I think it is reasonable to assume that any economy - be it the U.S. or Europe - cannot continue to have as rapid a growth as they have had in the past." \par \par \uc2  He said the Japanese economy is on the way up, predicting an upturn toward the second half of this year, but declined to comment on the direction of the U.S. economy. \par \par \uc2  Sakakibara repeated that he thinks the public works spending in a 16 trillion yen economic package announced earlier on Friday will have a far greater effect in boosting the economy than tax cuts. \par \par \uc2  Despite persistent calls, especially in the financial markets, for Japan to make stimulative income tax cuts permanent, he said it is a misconception to think they must be permanent to boost the economy. \par \par \uc2  He said the government will discuss the structure of the Japanese tax system including the rates of individual income tax and corporate tax. \par \par \uc2  But on income tax cuts, he said, "Whether this would lead to permanent tax cuts, at this moment, I cannot say." ((Tokyo newsroom 813-3432-8018 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du4o06qgv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
