\par\par \b \uc2  Dealers say dollar on rise despite Japan action. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Chikafumi Hodo \par \uc2 646 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 April 1998\par 08:45\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO (Reuters) - The Bank of Japan's (BOJ) persistent dollar-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  halted the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rise against the yen Friday, but that action alone may not be enough to push the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  off its upward path, dealers said Friday. \par \par \uc2  Without clear signs of improvement in the Japanese economy, the impact of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  may be limited and that should keep the dollar's long-term outlook bullish, they said. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was seen as an aggressive seller of dollars for yen in Tokyo Friday, keeping up its surprising efforts from New York Thursday, bankers said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar fell from a Tokyo high during the day of 131.55 yen to below 128 yen after what dealers said were persistent bouts of dollar sales for yen from late morning. \par \par \uc2  "It appeared that the BOJ wants to bring the dollar/yen lower. It (\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  overnight) didn't look like a price-smoothing \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but was aimed at taking the dollar down to some level," said Mitsuru Saito, deputy general manager of Treasury Department at Sanwa Bank. \par \par \uc2  "The BOJ's sole \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  may be able to cap the dollar for some time, but I don't think its effort can alter the basic bullishness of the dollar," Saito said. \par \par \uc2  The BOJ's action was taken ahead of Wednesday's Group of Seven (G7) industrialized nations' meeting in Washington, where \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  levels are expected to be discussed. It came also ahead of the Easter holidays in the United States and Europe, where the markets will be shut. \par \par \uc2  The BOJ shocked the market by \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  during New York trading Thursday. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  apparently first stepped in at around 133.00-133.50 yen and kept going all the way to under 130 yen in New York. \par \par \uc2  "I think the BOJ gave a pretty distinct signal to the market that it wanted the dollar to go lower against the yen," a senior Australian bank dealer said. "I don't know whether the yen can sustain these levels, but it was enough to have a psychological impact on the market." \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market three times on Thursday, buying yen for dollars in considerable amounts, an Economic Planning Agency official quoted BOJ Governor Masaru Hayami as saying Friday. \par \par \uc2  The dollar rebounded Thursday shortly after Prime Minister Ryutaro Hashimoto announced the government would pour 10 trillion yen ($78.1 billion) directly into stimulating the economy. It gained strength as the steps were not perceived to be bold enough to satisfy the market, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The prime minister told the nation that the government would put 10 trillion yen into direct fiscal stimulus, including four trillion yen in income tax cuts over two years, to revive the economy from its "severe state." \par \par \uc2  "The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  may only slow the pace of the dollar's rise, but the dollar is expected to be well-bid due to its sound fundamentals," said Yasuhisa Morikuni, assistant vice president at Bank of America (BOA). \par \par \uc2  "Without concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the dollar is unlikely to slide (against the yen)," Morikuni said. \par \par \uc2  U.S. Treasury Secretary Robert Rubin Thursday welcomed the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  but made no mention on whether the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2  would join in. \par \par \uc2  Separately, a senior Treasury official said that the U.S. administration has not changed its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy. \par \par \uc2  In the near term, the market will focus its attention on next week's G7 meeting to determine the trend of the dollar/yen, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "In the short term, the market will become cautious before the G7 meeting, but operators are still likely to buy dollars," said Sanwa's Saito said. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said that they want to see what Japan will bring back from the meeting, particularly after the tax cuts and economic steps unveiled Thursday. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du4a07ji0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
