\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS-U.S. welcomes Japan's  \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention \highlight\uc2 . \b0\par\par\uc2  By Knut Engelmann \par \uc2 419 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 April 1998\par 17:27\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  WASHINGTON, April 9 (Reuters) - U.S. Treasury Secretary Robert Rubin on Thursday welcomed Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to bolster the yen's value but made no mention whether the United States was joining the action. \par \par \uc2  Rubin's statement came as the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  said in Tokyo that it had bought yen and sold dollars to defend its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which has fallen recently on doubts over a weak economy. \par \par \uc2  "We share the concern expressed by the Japanese Prime Minister (Hashimoto) about recent weakness in the yen, and in that context we welcome the action taken by the Japanese authorities in the exchange market to support the value of the yen," Rubin said in a statement. \par \par \uc2  The dollar sales pushed the dollar's value below 130 yen. The dollar fell as low as 129.52 yen from 133.10/20 at the open and an overnight high of 133.62. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers said Rubin's support for the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  showed the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2  was committed to stopping the yen from falling in value and that was of greater significance to the market than the amount of dollars sold. \par \par \uc2  "The signal to the market is probably more important than the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  itself," said Merrill Lynch senior financial analyst David Horner. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2  last gave its verbal support to Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in December. Since then, U.S. officials have focused on Tokyo's failure to stimulate its ailing economy. \par \par \uc2  Tokyo's move to boost the yen came less than a week before a meeting in Washington of finance ministers from the Group of Seven top industrial nations, at which Japan's economy is expected to take center stage. \par \par \uc2  Rubin also gave a cautious welcome to an overnight announcement in Tokyo of long-awaited steps to boost Japan's ailing economy. \par \par \uc2  Japanese Prime Minister Ryutaro Hashimoto announced four trillion yen ($30.5 billion) in income tax cuts and 10 trillion yen ($76.3 billion) in extra spending. \par \par \uc2  "We look forward to seeing the details later this month," Rubin said, adding: "What is crucial is that Japan move quickly to put in place a strong program." \par \par \uc2  The United States has been openly pressing Japan to take strong action to boost its moribund economy to spur consumer spending and to permit Japan to take a greater role in helping reinvigorate other Asian economies in South Korea, Thailand and Indonesia, which are now receiving international bailouts. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du4907moh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
