\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar tumbles against the Japanese yen. [CORRECTED] \b0\par\par\uc2 646 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Januar 1998\par 23:59\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  By Al Yoon NEW YORK, Jan 7 (Reuters) - The dollar fell from a 5-3/4 year high (corrects from 5-3/4 year low) against the yen on Monday amid market speculation that Japan might garner the support of other industrialized nations to prop up the ailing yen. \par \par \uc2  The brisk selling of dollars for yen also depressed the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  against German mark. The dollar was trading off at 131.50 yen late in the day from 133.95 yen on Tuesday and a session high of 134.40. It was also lower against the mark at 1.8260 from 1.8320. \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders stepped back from earnest dollar-buying strategies in a cautious move ahead of news on talks between Japan's vice finance minister for international affairs, Eisuke Sakakibara, and U.S. finance officials. Concern was that Sakakibara, also known as "Mr. Yen" for his ability to verbally manipulate \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates, was on a mission to enlist the support of the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2  for a coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem the yen's slide. \par \par \uc2  Earlier, the dollar rose for a third consecutive session to its highest level against the yen since April 1992, before ending lower. "The erosion in dollar-yen is very much correlated to the official (Sakakibara) visit to Washington and the continuing cloud of potential \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which is causing some fear," said Diego Giurleo, vice president of Treasury at Royal Bank of Canada. The dollar bounced from lows late in the session after Sakakibara declined to comment when asked if there was a chance of concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to defend the yen. Separarely, Koji Omi, director general of Japan's Economic Planning Agency, told reporters that he did not discuss \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  with Greenspan. Asked about a chance of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to defend the yen, Omi said, "We will see the situation." Traders also said the pullback was a move by some to book profits after the dollar's runup from levels below 130 yen in December. Analysts said the Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and economic crisis would further weaken the yen, possibly to as low as 150 per dollar. "Problems in Southeast Asia are not going away," said John Gariano, vice president at Sumitomo Bank New York, who added that a plan to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  would do little to solve Japan's underlying problem of stagnant domestic growth. A string of all-time lows for Southeast Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  such as the Indonesian rupiah and Thai baht, amid fears those countries were balking at reforms required in international aid packages, also portended yen weakness, traders said. Some traders said the yen's tone improved following reports earlier in the week of stronger Japanese efforts to recapitalize its financial system by taking a stake in its troubled banks. Royal Bank of Canada's Giurleo noted that the market was wary of a steeper fall, remembering the dollar's slide from 127.46 yen to below 112 in about two weeks in May 1997. Against the mark, traders said higher U.S. interest rates and the relatively strong state of the economy would continue to underpin demand for dollars. Selling pressure against the yen overrode those forces for the time being, however, sending the dollar lower against the German \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . "Dollar-mark is trading on the sentiment of dollar-yen right now," Giurleo said. "It's still the best place to lodge your money in this world environment." Analysts also said the lack of protest from German \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials about recent mark weakness was seen as a green light to sell marks. In other trading, the dollar fell to 1.4785 Swiss francs from 1.4805 late on Tuesday and the British pound edged up to $1.6258 from $1.6245. The dollar was off at Canadian $1.4290 from C$1.4322.   (c) Reuters Limited 1998 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010926du17017h7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
