\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar/yen ends off lows in U.S. after Japan talk. \b0\par\par\uc2 637 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Januar 1998\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, Jan 7 (Reuters) - The dollar tumbled from better than 5-1/2 year highs against the yen on Monday amid market speculation that Japan might garner the support of other industrialized nations to prop the ailing yen, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The brisk selling of dollars for yen also weighed on the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the German mark. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders stepped back from their earnest dollar-buying strategies in a cautious move amid talks between Japan's Vice Finance Minister for International Affairs Eisuke Sakakibara and U.S. finance officials. Investment banks and funds were spotted liquidating long positions, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Concern that Sakakibara, also known as "Mr. Yen" for his ability to verbally manipulate \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates, was on a mission to enlist the support of the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2  for a coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem the yen's slide. \par \par \uc2  The dollar has hit its highest level against the yen since April 1992 for three consecutive sessions. \par \par \uc2  "The erosion in dollar/yen is very much correlated to the official (Japan) visit to Washington and the continuing cloud of potential \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  which is causing some fear in long holders of dollar/yen," said Diego Giurleo, vice president of Treasury at Royal Bank of Canada. \par \par \uc2  Dollar/yen ended lower at 131.55/65 on Wednesday from 132.50/60 at the open and its overnight high of 134.40. Dollar/mark eased to 1.8236/42 from 1.8255/60. \par \par \uc2  The dollar bounced from lows late in the session after Sakakibara declined comment after his meeting with Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan. Separately, Koji Omi, director general of Japan's Economic Planning Agency told reporters that he did not discuss \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  with the Fed chairman. \par \par \uc2  Asked about a chance of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to defend the yen, Omi said, "we will see the situation." \par \par \uc2  Traders also said the pullback was a profit-taking move after the runup from below 130 in December. Longer-term, however, analysts said Asian woes would sponsor a still-weaker yen and levels close to 150 have been bandied about. \par \par \uc2  "Problems in Southeast Asia are not going away," said John Gariano, vice president at Sumitomo Bank New York, who added that a plan to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  would do little to solve Japan's underlying problem of stagnant domestic growth. \par \par \uc2  A string of all-time lows for Southeast Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and fears some countries were balking at reforms required in international aid packages hurt the yen, traders said. \par \par \uc2  Some traders said the yen's tone improved following reports earlier in the week of stronger Japanese government efforts to recapitalize Japan's financial system by taking a stake in its troubled banks. \par \par \uc2  Giurleo noted that the market was wary of a steeper fall reminiscent of the dollar's rapid slide from 127.46 to below 112 last May. \par \par \uc2  Despite dollar/mark's softness on Wednesday, traders said higher U.S. interest rates and the relatively strong state of the economy would continue to underpin dollar demand. Brisk profit-taking in mark/yen padded dollar/mark losses, too. \par \par \uc2  "Dollar/mark is trading on the sentiment of dollar/yen right now," Giurleo said. "It's still the best place to lodge your money in this world environment." \par \par \uc2  Analysts also said the lack of protest from German \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials about recent mark weakness was seen as a green light to sell marks. \par \par \uc2  In other trading, the dollar ended higher at 1.4782/92 Swiss francs from 1.4760/70 at the open and was lower at Canadian $1.4293/98 from C$1.4315/20. Sterling edged up to $1.6255/65 from $1.6238/48 and the Australian dollar rose to $0.6420/25 from $0.6379/84. \par \par \uc2  ((--Al Yoon, 212-859-1672)) \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1998 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010926du17017gx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
