\par\par \b \uc2  Dlr resumes climb vs yen, ends US broadly higher. \b0\par\par\uc2 625 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 November 1997\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, Nov 13 (Reuters) - The dollar retraced some overnight losses and ended higher against the yen after the downgrade of three leading Japanese banks highlighted weak economic fundamentals and overshadowed rumors of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Dollar/mark, meanwhile, benefitted from a rebound in U.S. stocks and repeated profit-taking in the mark/yen cross. \par \par \uc2  The dollar fought back much of its losses versus the yen throughout the session as traders discounted rumors that the Bank of Japan was selling dollars in an effort to brake the sharp slide in the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  this month. \par \par \uc2  "Everyone was talking about (Bank of Japan) selling dollars but if they were it would be commercial sales, not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Chris Melendez, vice president and senior dealer at Sanwa Bank California. \par \par \uc2  "Sometimes the Bank of Japan will go fish around in Singapore and ask for prices, but it's all commercial related," he added. \par \par \uc2  The dollar, after trading over 126 yen for much of the session, ended at 125.70/80 from 125.24/29 at the open. It rose to 1.7255/65 marks from 1.7235/40. \par \par \uc2  Dealers had been on watch for any type of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2  or Japan in recent days, following a more than six-yen rise in dollar/yen this month. The lack of any verbal comments from officials had been taken as a green light to maintain the trend to sell yen for dollars and marks. \par \par \uc2  The silence continued on Thursday after Deputy Secretary of the Treasury Lawrence Summers said that U.S. policy toward exchange rates had not changed. \par \par \uc2  "The market can interpret the comments as some sort of tacit approval of the recent gains in dollar/yen," said Bob Lynch, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Paribas. \par \par \uc2  The midday downgrades of three Japanese banks by European rating agency IBCA renewed the bid under dollar/yen by putting the spotlight on weakened financial profiles amid a stagnant Japanese economy and recent stock market volatility. \par \par \uc2  Banks downgraded were Fuji Bank, Industrial Bank of Japan and Sakura Bank. Dai-Ichi Kangyo Bank, Sanwa Bank and Sumitomo Bank were placed on review for downgrade, IBCA said. \par \par \uc2  "(Japan) fundamentals dictate yen weakness over the next several months and until there's a significant shift in fundamentals the bias will be (negative for yen), but you'll have these modest pullbacks from time to time," said Lynch. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said comments from Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan, who said Southeast Asian market turmoil has had only modest impact on the U.S. economy but that the effect will not be negligible, were met with a slightly positive reaction by the dollar. \par \par \uc2  An 86-point gain in the Dow industrials average following a 157-point drop on Wednesday aided the dollar's rise against the mark, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Traders said heightened tension between the U.S. and Iraq over the expulsion of U.S. members of a U.N arms inspection team also sparked some broad dollar buying. \par \par \uc2  Iraq's decision followed a resolution from the U.N. Security council on Wednesday condemning such action. President Bill Clinton called Iraq's decision "clearly unacceptable and a challenge to the international community." \par \par \uc2  Mark/yen profit taking by large funds to below the 73 level supported dollar/mark above 1.7170, traders said. \par \par \uc2  In other trading, the dollar rose to 1.4010/15 Swiss francs from 1.3998/08 at the open and edged higher to Canadian $1.4085/95 from C$1.4073/78. \par \par \uc2  The British pound fell to $1.6970/80 from $1.6977/87. \par \par \uc2  The Australian dollar eased to $0.6971/76 from $0.6981/86. \par \par \uc2  ((--Al Yoon, 212-859-1672)) \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011003dtbd02l6h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
