\par\par \b \uc2  POLL - Dollar to drop over next twelve months. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Pratima Desai \par \uc2 649 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 August 1997\par 11:31\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Aug 7 (Reuter) - The dollar is expected to fall against the mark and the yen over the next 12 months as central banks rebuff attempts to drive the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  higher, a Reuters survey of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategists across the world showed. \par \par \uc2  In the survey of 55 contributors, the median forecast showed the dollar at 1.88 marks and 118 yen at the end of this month. By end-July 1998, the dollar is expected to drop to 1.7550 marks and 110 yen. \par \par \uc2  The dollar closed at 1.8795 marks and 119.20 yen on August 5, the final day of the survey. \par \par \uc2  Peter Luxton, director of global \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  at Standard & Poors' MMS in London, said he expected the central banks of Germany, Japan and the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2  to use \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to push the dollar down. \par \par \uc2  "The Bundebank is keeping quiet, but mark weakness risks importing inflation into Germany. They may be trying to catch the market out, long and wrong," Luxton said. "Levels above 120 yen are sensitive to U.S. criticism because of on-going trade friction." \par \par \uc2  In an effort to redress the huge bilateral trade imbalance, the U.S. and Japanese authorities allowed the dollar to fall to a post World War II low of 79.70 yen on April 30, 1995. \par \par \uc2  Inflation imported because of the weak mark, and signs that the German economy may be coming back to life mean the Bundesbank will start thinking about higher interest rates, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  The German \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  fixed the repo at an unchanged three percent in mid-July for two weeks only ahead of its four-week recess. \par \par \uc2  This has prompted speculation that the bank might opt for a variable repo rate tender in the second half of its summer break. \par \par \uc2  "There is the possibility of a dual repo, half fixed and half variable. If this happens the markets will expect higher interest rates," said Nick Shamin, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at ANZ Investment Bank in London. \par \par \uc2  Andrea Zuccheri, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Sella Asset Management in Milan, believes the dollar will stabilise against the mark at current levels. \par \par \uc2  "It is unlikely the Bundesbank will allow the mark to fall much further. Neither do we believe the Bundesbank will nudge interest rates higher," Zuccheri said. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar will remain strong until the new European single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ...comes into being. The uncertainty will disappear once the conversion rates are decided." \par \par \uc2  Expectations that European economic and monetary  union (EMU) will go ahead on time in January 1999 with a broad membership, has been the major factor behind mark weakness. \par \par \uc2  The expected inclusion in the euro of historically volatile \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  such as the Italian lira has prompted many investors to move into dollar assets. \par \par \uc2  The lowest one-month prediction in the poll for the dollar was 1.7700 marks, and the highest was 1.9250 marks. \par \par \uc2  Analysts who see the dollar continuing to move higher say good economic growth and subdued inflationary pressures in the United States are the main reason. \par \par \uc2  "The U.S. economy is in good shape and there is a dissatisfaction with everything European. The dollar will be bought against everything for the rest of this year," said Alan Collins, head of technical analysis at Merita Bank in London. \par \par \uc2  The one-month forecast range for the dollar versus the yen was 114 to 122. \par \par \uc2  The continuing weakness in the Japanese economy and the prospect of no change in monetary policy means that the interest rate differential will remain in favour of the dollar, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  With the Federal funds rate at 5.5 percent and the Japanese discount rate at 0.5 percent, the carry costs of a short dollar position are too high, they said. \par \par \uc2  -- London Polling Unit:+44 171 542 2737 \par \par \uc2  email:polling.unit@rtrlondon.co.uk \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011002dt8701g6l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
