\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar climbs on interest rate outlook. \b0\par\par\uc2 647 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 April 1997\par 23:14\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, April 14 - The dollar rose on Monday amid expectations that interest rates will remain low in Japan and Europe and move higher in the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  But the possibility of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem the dollar's rise and uncertainty before the release of a crucial inflation report on Tuesday kept the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from rising even higher. \par \par \uc2  Remarks by Bank of Japan Governor Yasuo Matsushita on Monday reaffirmed the view that rock-bottom rates in Japan are not about to turn higher. Matsushita told a group of business leaders in Tokyo that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would continue to conduct monetary policy with an emphasis on strengthening Japan's economic recovery. \par \par \uc2  In September 1995, the Bank of Japan cut its official discount rate to a record low 0.5 percent to bolster Japan's struggling economy. \par \par \uc2  "I think what's propelling the dollar just fractionally higher is you're rewarded for owning dollars on a short-term basis by the interest rate differential," said John McCarthy, senior vice president at ING Capital Markets. \par \par \uc2  "The general feeling is rates will continue to move up and rates in Europe and Japan are, at best, static for the moment," he added. \par \par \uc2  With the dollar trading near its highest level in more than 4-1/2 years against the yen, traders remained wary that the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Japan could \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to prop up the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  On Saturday, the Japanese daily Mainichi Shimbun reported that Japanese Finance Minister Hiroshi Mitsuzuka told cabinet ministers on Friday that Japan will act in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets when the dollar rises to 127 yen to 128 yen. But when questioned by reporters on Monday, Mitsuzuka denied having mentioned a specific exchange rate. \par \par \uc2  Indeed, some traders briefly sold dollars on Monday afternoon on news that Treasury Secretary Robert Rubin said the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2  would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  "when it's appropriate and not act when it's not appropriate." \par \par \uc2  Analysts, however, were careful not to read too much into Rubin's remarks. \par \par \uc2  "Our own feeling here in New York is that Mr. Rubin is a trader, so he knows the trader's mentality," said David Ethridge, director of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analysis at MMS International in North America. \par \par \uc2  "By stating this kind of 'on-the-one-hand' type of approach, (Rubin) keeps the market guessing," Ethridge said. \par \par \uc2  Rubin was formerly a co-chairman of Goldman Sachs & Co. \par \par \uc2  Shortly after the impact from the Rubin statements faded from the market, the dollar soared to 1.7310 marks, its highest level in more than three years. Traders attributed the late surge primarily to dollar purchases by a large Wall Street investment bank. \par \par \uc2  In late New York trading, the dollar settled at 1.7310 marks, up from 1.7205 marks late Friday. The dollar climbed to 126.63 yen from 126.12 yen. \par \par \uc2  The dollar also climbed to 1.4725 Swiss francs from 1.4610 francs, and to Canadian $1.3995 from C$1.3983. The British pound fell to $1.6205 from $1.6275. \par \par \uc2  On Tuesday, the Labour Department will release a widely watched report on consumer price inflation. \par \par \uc2  Market participants are very sensitive to any signs of higher inflation that could cause the Federal Reserve to hike interest rates again. \par \par \uc2  "I expect that what people are looking for is for CPI to confirm that growth is still pretty strong and to suggest a rate hike either at the May or July meeting" of the Federal Reserve's Open Market Committee, said Cynthia Cooley, a \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst and trader at United Gulf Bank of Bahrain in Boston. \par \par \uc2  Fed policy-makers are scheduled to meet on May 20 and again on July 1 and 2. On March 25, the Fed hiked the federal funds rate to 5.50 percent from 5.25 percent. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011002dt4e070h0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
