\par\par \b \uc2  Dlr ends higher in US amid rate differential talk. \b0\par\par\uc2 690 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 April 1997\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, April 14 (Reuter) - The dollar closed higher amid expectations for continued low interest rates in Japan and Europe and higher rates in the U.S., experts said. \par \par \uc2  Yet the possibility of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem the dollar's rise plus uncertainty before the release of a crucial inflation report on Tuesday introduced a note of caution into \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. \par \par \uc2  "I think what's propelling the dollar just fractionally higher is you're rewarded for owning dollars on a short-term basis by the interest rate differential," said John McCarthy, senior vice president at ING Capital Markets. \par \par \uc2  "The general feeling is (U.S.) rates will continue to move up and rates in Europe and Japan are, at best, static for the moment," McCarthy added. \par \par \uc2  Remarks by the Bank of Japan (BOJ) Governor Yasuo Matsushita on Monday reaffirmed the view that rockbottom rates in Japan are not about to turn higher. \par \par \uc2  Matsushita told a group of business leaders in Tokyo that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would continue to conduct monetary policy with an emphasis on strengthening Japan's economic recovery. \par \par \uc2  The dollar closed at 126.53/63 yen and 1.7302/07 marks, up from 126.13/23 yen and 1.7210/20 marks at the open. \par \par \uc2  With the dollar trading near its highest level in more than four and a half years against the yen, traders remained wary that the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Japan could \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to prop up the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  On Saturday, Japanese daily newspaper, Mainichi Shimbun, reported that Japanese Finance Minister Hiroshi Mitsuzuka told cabinet ministers on Friday that Japan will act in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets if the dollar rises to 127 to 128 yen. \par \par \uc2  But when questioned by reporters on Monday, Mitsuzuka denied having mentioned a specific exchange rate at which the BOJ would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell dollars for yen. \par \par \uc2  Indeed, some traders briefly sold dollars during Monday afternoon following news that Treasury Secretary Robert Rubin said the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2  will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  "when it's appropriate and not act when it's not appropriate." \par \par \uc2  Analysts, however, were careful not to read too much into the Treasury Secretary's remarks. \par \par \uc2  "Our own feeling here in New York is that Mr. Rubin is a trader, so he knows the trader's mentality," said David Ethridge, director of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analysis at MMS International in North America. "By stating this kind of 'on-the-one-hand' type of approach, (Rubin) keeps the market guessing." \par \par \uc2  Rubin was formerly a co-chairman of Goldman Sachs and Co. \par \par \uc2  Shortly after the impact from the Rubin statements faded from the market, the dollar soared to 1.7310 marks, its highest level in more than three years. Traders attributed the late surge primarily to dollar purchases by a large Wall Street investment bank. \par \par \uc2  On Tuesday, The Labor Department will release the consumer price index (CPI) for March. Economists polled by Reuters expected the CPI rose 0.2 percent in March. Excluding food and energy items, CPI is seen up 0.3 percent. \par \par \uc2  "I expect that what people are looking for is for CPI to confirm that growth is still pretty strong and to suggest a rate hike either at the May or July meeting," said Cynthia Cooley, a \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst and trader at United Gulf Bank of Bahrain in Boston. \par \par \uc2  Fed policymakers are scheduled to meet on May 20 and again on July 1 and 2. \par \par \uc2  The dollar ended at 1.4705/15 Swiss francs and Canadian $1.3994/99, up from 1.4652/62 francs and C$1.3975/80 at the open. Sterling fell to $1.6200/05 from $1.6230/40. The Australian dollar rose to $0.7795/00 from $0.7785/90. \par \par \uc2  Analysts also noted concern in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market on Monday about the possibility that stocks might selloff again after the Dow industrials index tumbled some 148 points on Friday. Initially, stock prices did decline on Monday. But the Dow industrials recovered late in the session to finish up 60 points at 6451.90. \par \par \uc2  ((-- Donna Sells 212-859-1668)) \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011002dt4e070gd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
