\par\par \b \uc2  Haiti democracy could be unraveling - diplomats. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Julie Vorman \par \uc2 643 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 November 1995\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1995 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  PORT-AU-PRINCE, Nov 29 (Reuter) - Haiti's tenuous hold on democracy shows signs of unravelling as the country chafes under the tutelage of its main ally, the United States, diplomats and analysts said on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  With a presidential election just 18 days away, little campaigning is evident, inflation is pushing the price of beans and rice out of reach for many Haitians, and foreign aid has slowed to a trickle because of President Jean-Bertrand Aristide's reluctance to move on economic reforms. \par \par \uc2  Since rioters burned down the police station in the capital's largest slum last week, few Haitian or international police have ventured back into the neighbourhood. \par \par \uc2  Western diplomats said the relationship between Haiti and the United States, never comfortable, has deteriorated. \par \par \uc2  "I have spoken with both sides. I don't think it's an easy time for either side," one diplomat said, speaking on condition of anonymity. "In the United States, they have their calendar and they want things to move forward. And at the same time, things are not easy on the ground and Aristide has to manage this three-year thing." \par \par \uc2  The diplomat was referring to a call last week by Aristide supporters for him to stay in office for three more years to make up for time he spent in exile after a 1991 military coup interrupted his five-year term in office. \par \par \uc2  Aristide indicated at first he might stay, sparking a near-crisis in the U.S.-Haiti relationship, but he sought to dispel any doubt on Tuesday, saying he would leave as scheduled next Feb. 7. He called a meeting of the three government branches on Wednesday to clarify his position but parliament and supreme court leaders refused to attend. \par \par \uc2  In another area of friction with Washington, Aristide supporters are upset that the United States has not yet returned thousands of pages of Haitian military documents seized during the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the island in 1994. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2  is concerned about the prospect of another huge wave of boat people trying to escape Haiti's poverty. \par \par \uc2  On Wednesday, the U.S. Coast Guard repatriated a second wave of 577 Haitian migrants who were intercepted as they sailed toward Florida in several ships. U.S. officials said the exodus of about 1,000 people last week was triggered by Haiti's faltering economy and was the largest since Aristide was returned to power by multinational forces in October 1994. \par \par \uc2  But embassy spokesman Stan Schrager said there was always "an upward surge ... at this time of the year," adding: "As of right now, I don't think we can conclusively say that we have a boat people problem or that this is a trend." \par \par \uc2  To inject more Haitian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  into the market, the government has reduced its foreign reserves by an estimated $20 million in the past month. This week alone, the government is releasing an estimated $1 million each day, according to an economist close to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Aristide, a charismatic former priest, lost credibility with potential investors when his prime minister, Smarck Michel, resigned last month in frustration over the slow pace of economic reforms. \par \par \uc2  U.S. Secretary of State Warren Christopher later sent a cable to the U.S. Embassy in Port-au-Prince warning of a plot by former dictator Gen. Prosper Avril to launch attacks on supporters of Aristide's Lavalas movement. \par \par \uc2  High-ranking Haitain officials say they were never informed of the cable, and U.S. failure to transmit the security warnings has further strained relations. \par \par \uc2  On Nov. 7, Jean-Hubert Feuille, a cousin of Aristide and member of Haiti's legislature, was gunned down in a Port-au-Prince suburb, although officials are unwilling to say whether Avril was behind the attack. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1995 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011103drbt01bbu\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
