\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar gets needed boost from Germany's Tietmeyer. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Marjorie Olster \par \uc2 632 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Oktober 1995\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1995 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, Oct 24 (Reuter) - The dollar got a much-needed boost from German \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  president Hans Tietmeyer, who said recent \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements were exaggerated and voiced willingness to join concerted market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Before Tietmeyer spoke, the dollar was already rebounding from an 11-week low against the mark Monday, helped partly by a pause in the mark's explosive rally recently against weaker European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  like the French franc and Italian lira. \par \par \uc2  "It was definitely dollar supportive," Debra Larsen, assistant vice president and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer at Commerzbank, said of the Tietmeyer comments. \par \par \uc2  "We have to be careful about \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . If we start to breach levels central banks are not comfortable with, we'll know it and it will be expensive for people short dollars," she added. \par \par \uc2  Tietmeyer said temporary exaggerations can occur in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market "as is again the case at present" in the dollar's stumble against the mark recently. \par \par \uc2  "We are willing to participate in sensible international cooperation in the future. Benign neglect was never our policy," Tietmeyer said. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said Tietmeyer's comments appeared to be aimed at talking down the mark's value and reversing the perception that, in comments last week, the Bundesbank president indicated he is not concerned about an overvalued mark. \par \par \uc2  It was also seen as a warning to \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  speculators betting the dollar will slide further. \par \par \uc2  "The market was caught very much by surprise by his actual comments," said Peggy Reed, vice president of institutional \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  sales at Credit Agricole. \par \par \uc2  The dollar rose to 1.3918 marks from 1.3858 late Monday. It traded as high as 1.3970 marks just after the Tietmeyer comments. Against the yen, the dollar was up to 100.38 from 99.80 Monday. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the market is always on guard for \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  when the dollar falls sharply as it did Friday and Monday. Those fears can often limit the dollar's downside. \par \par \uc2  The Bundesbank last participated in concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  with the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Japan to shore up the dollar on August 15. They surprised the market and the dollar rose from 1.43 marks to 1.47 marks. \par \par \uc2  Reed said she believed Tietmeyer's speech was aimed at a domestic audience concerned the mark's strength could hamper an export-led recovery. A strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  makes a country's exports more expensive for foreign consumers. \par \par \uc2  Bundesbank board member Edgar Meister said in a speech Tuesday that the persistently strong mark had helped worsen the environment for German exporters. \par \par \uc2  Analysts did not see the Tietmeyer comments as a hint at further cuts in German interest rates. A rate cut in Germany could weaken the mark by making German assets relatively less attractive. \par \par \uc2  Tietmeyer said easier German monetary policy would not solve exchange rate problems and stable domestice monetary policy would be the base for "stable and appropriate exchange rates." \par \par \uc2  He also cautioned \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  crises can only be solved permananently if countries tackle what he called "uncertainties about political decisions taken by some of our partner nations." \par \par \uc2  Reed said this was an indication Germany views other countries as responsible for recent \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  volatility. \par \par \uc2  "The Germans don't feel they are the problem. He has appealed to the French. He will appeal to the Italians again and he will talk to the U.S," said Reed. \par \par \uc2  Dealers predicted the dollar's move up will only be sustained if Tietmeyer's comments are followed by similar statements from U.S. officials or by actual \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Otherwise, they expect the rally to fizzle. \par \par \uc2  In other trade, the dollar rose to 1.1345 Swiss francs from 1.1260 Monday. It fell to Canadian $1.3665 from $1.3695. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1995 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011103drao02p4g\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
