\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar ends lower as German mark strengthens. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Anissa Wells \par \uc2 634 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 September 1995\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1995 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK (Reuter) - The dollar edged lower against most leading \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  Thursday as traders bought German marks amid concerns about developments in Europe. \par \par \uc2  Traders said nervousness about the future of the European economic and monetary union (EMU) prompted many investors to dump \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  like the Italian lira and the Spanish peseta in favour of the German mark, which is one of Europe's most stable \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Traders noted that as the mark rose against its European counterparts, it had an adverse affect on the dollar. \par \par \uc2  "Every time the mark makes new headway against the crosses like the lira and the French franc, the dollar takes a hit," said Richard Vullo, vice president at Bayerische Hypothenken und Wechsel Bank AG in New York. "People are just unsure about what to make of things going on over there in Europe. There is just so much confusion about EMU that we are continuing to see this flight to quality," into the mark. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the central banks of Italy, Portugal and possibly France were rumoured to be \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to defend their respective \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which in turn led to some volatile dollar trading. \par \par \uc2  The dollar fell as low as 1.4170 marks in early trading. By late New York trading, it settled in at 1.4218 German marks, down 1.4243 marks late Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was at 99.45 Japanese yen, down from 100.43 yen late Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  The flap over monetary union in Europe began last week when German Finance Minister Theo Waigel told a closed-door session of the German parliament that Italy would not be among the first wave of countries forming a single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Analysts said there is now widespread concern that several other European countries may also fail to make the cut. \par \par \uc2  "There is the general fear that EMU may not happen at all," said Amy Smith, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at consulting firm I.D.E.A. "But whatever the case, the EMU situation is pressuring the dollar quite a bit and the general thinking is that this is probably going to be a long-term problem." \par \par \uc2  The dollar was further pressured on Thursday as traders began selling dollars for yen in anticipation of a large-scale demand for the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  after the "fixing" in Tokyo on Friday at the Japanase fiscal half-year. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the fixing is mainly for bookkeeping purposes and it allows Japan to "fix" or lock in a certain exchange rate for valuation of foreign holdings. \par \par \uc2  Many traders believe the Bank of Japan will make an effort to keep the dollar around 100 yen ahead of the fixing, but some say that the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will not likely interfere with the yen buying that is expected to ensue after the fixing. \par \par \uc2  "The recent euphoria about the dollar is coming to an end," said one trader at a large Japanese bank, referring to the elation over the dollar's recent rise above 104 yen, due mostly to aggressive prodding by the Bank of Japan. \par \par \uc2  "The reality is people do not necessarily want to hold dollars and we will probably see that sentiment reflected after the fixing," the trader added. \par \par \uc2  Indeed, many traders believe that if the so-called "Group of Three" economic powerhouses -- the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Germany and Japan -- fail to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the dollar's behalf soon, the ongoing strength of the mark and the renewed demand for yen will likely cause the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to grind much lower. \par \par \uc2  In other trading, the dollar eased to 1.1465 Swiss francs, down from 1.1528 late Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  The British pound was at $1.5793, up slightly from $1.5790 Wednesday, while the dollar rose to Canadian $1.3512 from C$1.3487. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1995 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011103dr9s03hhp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
