\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar tumbles as rally on German rate cut fizzles. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Caren Bohan \par \uc2 581 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 August 1995\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1995 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK (Reuter) - The dollar tumbled from a six-month high Thursday after a rally prompted by German interest rate cuts gave way to severe profit-taking. \par \par \uc2  The failure to reach higher levels was seen as a disappointing signal for the dollar, which has been in an uptrend for the past several weeks. \par \par \uc2  "It's almost frightening," said Ed Whitmore, vice president at Standard Chartered Bank. He said the dollar should have been able to sustain the rally, given that the German rate cuts were unexpectedly generous. \par \par \uc2  By late in the New York session, the dollar had fallen to 1.4769 German marks from Wednesday's 1.4834 marks. The dollar was at 96.58 Japanese yen, down from 96.75 yen Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Following its regular biweekly council meeting, the Bundesbank, Germany's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , slashed half a point off of its two official interest rates, the discount rate and the Lombard emergency lending rate. The discount rate was chopped to 3.5 percent and the Lombard rate to 5.5 percent. \par \par \uc2  Such a move would ordinarily tend to bolster the dollar by diminishing the attractiveness of deposits based in marks. Indeed, the initial reaction in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market was to take the dollar sharply higher. \par \par \uc2  But a surprisingly weak report on American durable goods orders, released shortly after the Bundesbank news, triggered a sell-off just after the dollar hit a six-month high of 1.4990 marks. \par \par \uc2  The Commerce Department reported that orders for big ticket items, such as major appliances, fell 1.7 percent -- surprising many economists who had forecast a 0.8 percent gain in orders for the month. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar got a rise in the first hour after the Bundesbank move, but then, when the durable goods report was released, it dropped like a stone," said Carl Weinberg, chief economist at High Frequency Economics. \par \par \uc2  "People took this report as a sign that the Fed would be more likely to cut rates," Weinberg said. "In that sense, it was a negative for the dollar. No one is thinking the Fed is going to cut rates anytime soon, but this would raise the likelihood of a cut down the road." \par \par \uc2  In the aftermath of the durable goods report, the dollar's slide accelerated as it fell through important technical levels, where standing orders to sell dollars were activated. \par \par \uc2  Weinberg noted that an important factor helping to support the dollar in the past several weeks was a well-timed \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  undertaken by the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Germany and Japan on Aug. 15. \par \par \uc2  A few bouts of joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Japan have also played a role. \par \par \uc2  Weinberg said the dollar's performance Thursday cast some doubt on its ability to rally without \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "It's amazing to me that absent official \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the dollar hasn't been able to make any gains against the mark and the yen," Weinberg said. "It leads you to believe there isn't a natural buoyancy there." \par \par \uc2  Still, many analysts remained optimistic. \par \par \uc2  "This dollar rally is legitimate and we are ultimately going to go higher," said John Rothfield, international economist at Nationsbanc-CRT. \par \par \uc2  In other late trade, the dollar stood at 1.2204 Swiss francs, down from 1.2265 Swiss francs. \par \par \uc2  The British pound traded at $1.5445, up from $1.5427, while the dollar slid to Canadian $1.3530 from C$1.3581. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1995 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011103dr8o04qdw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
