\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar ends mixed, waits for Fed, Bundesbank meetings. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Caren Bohan \par \uc2 612 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 August 1995\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1995 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK (Reuter) - The dollar ended mixed Monday after trading in a narrow range, as traders awaited key meetings by the Federal Reserve and Germany's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  over interest rate policy. \par \par \uc2  The Fed's policy-making committee meets on Tuesday to consider possible changes to the federal funds rate, now at 5.75 percent, and the discount rate, which stands at 5.25 percent. \par \par \uc2  Germany's Bundesbank, its \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , is scheduled to meet Thursday and has an opportunity to adjust its discount rate and Lombard emergency lending rate. \par \par \uc2  While most \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders see only an outside chance that the central banks will alter rates, the prospect of a surprise action is keeping the market on hold. \par \par \uc2  "Right now, (traders) have to be a little reserved just to be prudent because the Fed can always surprise you," said Kevin Harris, international economist at consulting firm MCM \b \highlight22\uc2 Currencywatch\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  In late New York trade, the dollar slipped to 96.87 yen from 97.42 yen on Friday. It firmed to 1.4767 German marks from late Friday's 1.4752. \par \par \uc2  "We'll have to go past the FOMC meeting to the Bundesbank meeting for people to start pushing things," Harris added. \par \par \uc2  The Fed last changed policy in early July, when it cut interest rates for the first time in three years. The Fed was reacting to signs of slowing growth, but since then the economy has renewed its momentum. \par \par \uc2  However, many traders are wondering whether the Fed will follow up its last rate cut with another one soon, since the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  tends to move in increments. \par \par \uc2  At the same time, Bundesbank officials have recently hinted at a willingness to allow German rates to fall gradually, although most analysts the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  might hold off until September. \par \par \uc2  Any Fed rate cut has the chance to prompt some selling of dollars, particularly if it were not followed up by German rate reductions. Lower rates rates can diminish the attractiveness of a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  by lowering the return on short-term securities. \par \par \uc2  "If the Fed were to cut rates I think you could see the dollar give back a good portion of its recent gains," said Peggy Reed, vice president at Credit Agricole. \par \par \uc2  The focus on monetary policy this week has shifted attention away from last week's concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the dollar by the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Germany and Japan. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  prompted a powerful rally in the dollar that took it to six-month highs against the mark and yen. Sentiment toward the dollar remains overwhelmingly positive in the aftermath. \par \par \uc2  "The market has gotten a very clear signal from the central banks that they do indeed want a higher dollar," said Alex Scarsini, director of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at Barclays de Zoete Wedd. Because of that, he said, "the market is really in a dollar-buying mode. People will continue to buy dollars on dips regardless of what happens with interest rates. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the dollar was pressured somewhat against the yen after two major U.S. credit-rating agencies, Moody's Investors Service Inc. and Standard & Poor's Corp, expressed concern about the health of several Japanese banks. \par \par \uc2  There were some fears that the Japanese banks might face liquidity problems that could force them to liquidate overseas holdings, such as U.S. Treasuries, leading to dollar selling. \par \par \uc2  In other late trade, the dollar stood at 1.2253 Swiss francs, up from 1.2232 Swiss francs. \par \par \uc2  The British pound traded at $1.5400, down from $1.5410, while the dollar firmed to Canadian C$1.3592 from C$1.3585. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1995 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011103dr8l04ui9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
