\par\par \b \uc2  U.S. finance,  \highlight22\uc2 currency \highlight\uc2  policy not acceptable - Kohl. \b0\par\par\uc2 749 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 April 1995\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1995 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BONN, April 20 (Reuter) - German Chancellor Helmut Kohl told German ARD television that current U.S. financial and \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies are not acceptable, ARD reported on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Kohl's remarks appeared to reflect growing frustration among major industrialised nations with the lack of U.S. action to support its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- which has fallen 12 percent against the deutschemark and 19 percent against the yen this year. \par \par \uc2  According to advance excerpts of the interview to be broadcast later on Thursday, Kohl said he would advise U.S. President Bill Clinton "not to let the dollar sink". \par \par \uc2  ARD added that Kohl had said U.S. financial and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies were "not acceptable" and that the U.S. must get its budget deficit in order. \par \par \uc2  His comments came on the same day that Bundesbank president Hans Tietmeyer told a news conference that countries seeking to strengthen their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  must pursue credible anti-inflation policies and demonstrate budgetary discipline. \par \par \uc2  The head of the German \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  did not directly criticise the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2  but said setting exchange rate targets or \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets would not work if countries failed to gain the confidence of the markets. \par \par \uc2  German manufacturers, especially in the key automobile and aircraft-building sectors, say that in the long term the strength of the mark is likely to hurt their competitiveness in international markets. \par \par \uc2  An overvalued mark would raise the cost of their goods to foreign customers. \par \par \uc2  An international monetary source in Tokyo said Japan wants the Group of Seven (G7) leading industrialised countries to send a clear signal on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  after its planned Washington meeting next Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The dollar rebounded slightly on international financial markets following Kohl's remarks. Traders said the tone of the remarks was unusually blunt, but the content unsurprising. REUTER \par \par \uc2  By David Crossland \par \par \uc2  FRANKFURT, April 20 (Reuter) - The Bundesbank on Thursday poured cold water on hopes of concerted Group of Seven action to end the turbulence on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, saying nations with weak \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  had to help themselves. \par \par \uc2  But German chancellor Helmut Kohl later made clear in a television interview, to be broadcast later on Thursday, that he believed U.S. finance and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy was unacceptable. \par \par \uc2  According to an advance release, Kohl said he would advise U.S. President Bill Clinton "not to let the dollar sink" and to bring the country's budget deficit back under control. \par \par \uc2  Tietmeyer told the news conference that correct policy was the key to ultimate market support. \par \par \uc2  "Stability-oriented policies have a positive impact in the long term. Markets in the end always follow such policies," Tietmeyer said. But he repeated the Bundesbank's line that exchange rate movements were currently exaggerated. \par \par \uc2  The mark has soared to record highs against at least eight \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , including the dollar, in the past few weeks while the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has come under severe pressure against the yen, notching up successive record lows. \par \par \uc2  Rate cuts by the Bundesbank last month and the Bank of Japan last week failed to strengthen the dollar, but European officials, at least, are confident the pressure will be off the Bundesbank in Washington to lower interest rates again. \par \par \uc2  As expected, the Bundesbank decided to keep its official rates unchanged at Thursday's central council meeting. \par \par \uc2  Addressing the question of how to boost IMF funding, Tietmeyer reiterated Germany opposition to a general allocation of Special Drawing Rights but said it may be prepared to accept a special SDR allocation targeted at IMF members that needed it. \par \par \uc2  "The Pandora's box of a general SDR allocation should not be opened because we see no global need," Tietmeyer said. \par \par \uc2  He said Germany was in favour of "conditional and temporary aid programmes and not a generous provision of liquidity." \par \par \uc2  Next week's meeting is unlikely to lead to a decision on the matter, Tietmeyer said. \par \par \uc2  He told the news conference the Bundesbank's profit plunged to 10.9 billion marks in 1994 from a record 18.83 billion in 1993, primarily because interest income fell in line with declining official interest rates last year. \par \par \uc2  The falling dollar also took its toll on Bundesbank earnings, forcing it to make writedowns of 2.8 billion marks on its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves in 1994. \par \par \uc2  Tietmeyer said the Bundesbank considered revising its balance sheet to take account of the weaker dollar but had decided against this, in part because it felt such a move would be misunderstood by the markets. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1995 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011103dr4k08mdj\par }\par \b \uc2 Zusammenfassung der Suche\b0\par \par  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdr